    "prologue": "<p>A community's courage does not depend on being described as invincible. Its rulers do not become just in every action because the community's scripture teaches justice. A massacre does not need a perfectly known death count before its victims deserve remembrance. These distinctions are obvious until pride or grief makes them inconvenient.</p><p>The Sikh story includes resistance, persecution, changing alliances, confederacies and a powerful kingdom. Each requires its own evidence. It is possible to recognise religious meaning in that history without using religion as an automatic explanation of every decision. Land, revenue, succession and military opportunity also mattered. A state is an institution exercising power over people, including people who do not share its ruler's faith.</p><p>This part therefore refuses two easy portraits: a spotless empire and a community reduced to violence. Neither allows us to see the people, decisions and institutions. The stronger account recognises real achievement and real limits. It also refuses inherited guilt. A seventeenth-century official, an eighteenth-century raider and a present-day neighbour are not one interchangeable person.</p><p class=\"w-signature\">Lovepreet Singh · MisterLove</p><blockquote class=\"w-pullquote\"><p>Truthful living gives truth a place in conduct.</p><cite>Author's explanatory paraphrase of the emphasis on truthful conduct, Siri Raag, Guru Nanak Dev Ji, ang 62. <a class=\"w-cite\" href=\"https://www.srigranth.org/servlet/gurbani.gurbani?Action=Page&amp;Param=62\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Sri Guru Granth Sahib Ji, Siri Raag, Guru Nanak Dev Ji, ang 62\">[Source 1]</a></cite></blockquote>",
